    {
      "id": "1570346382118879243",
      "type": "Default",
      "timestamp": "2026-11-12T07:55:53.122+00:00",
      "timestampEdited": null,
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "Hi! So far, we've only tested 4 chips, but they contained quite a few eFuses, so we can be fairly sure the eFuse works. I hope to get more proper characterization results in the future when Zedulo tests more chips, including retention/bake, but I'm afraid that won't happen soon.\n1. It runs from the regular 5V rail as long as enough current is supplied. The maximum current during programming is about 15 mA per blown fuse, so you should make sure not to blow too many at once, I actually recommend to do it bit-by-bit. I'm yet to publish a dual-voltage version (3.3V interface and 5V supply for writing).\n2. Unfortunately, there is no data for the actual resistance spread. To measure that, a dedicated test chip is required. The only read errors we got were the bits that were intentionally current-starved during programming by blowing 32 bits at the same time, which is beyond the chip's supply capabilities. Such bits were stuck in the \"underprogrammed\" state with different readings. For some of them it was possible to program them a second time to bring to a steady \"1\", and for some not.\n3. Not yet. The only data is that chips retained all programmed bits for about a month and that two chips surpassed 5 million reads for a bunch of fuses without error.\n4. It passes DRC as-is, nothing special is required.",
      "author": {
        "id": "709384666909507634",
        "name": "egorxe",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "Egor Lukyanchenko",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [],
        "avatarUrl": "2026-11_media/3-FB033.png"
      },
      "attachments": [],
      "embeds": [],
      "stickers": [],
      "reactions": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "inlineEmojis": []
    },
